<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanian Civil War (1432–1438)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1432">1432</a>–<a href="/years/1438">1438</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lithuanian Civil War of 1432–1438 was fought over the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a> and Sigismund Kęstutaitis, who was backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Sigismund's victory at Pabaiskas (Wiłkomierz) in 1435 decided the war in his favour and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in its union with Poland, though Sigismund himself was murdered in 1440.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanian-civil-war-1432-1438.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Civil War (1432–1438)" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orionus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vilkamir_battle_monument_%E2%80%94_%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%BD%D1%96%D0%BA_%D0%B1%D1%96%D1%82%D0%B2%D1%8B_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%B4_%D0%92%D1%96%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC.Jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a> died on 27 October 1430 without an heir or a will; his only grandson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II of Moscow</a>, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and could not lead the Catholic Grand Duchy. The Lithuanian nobles elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a> without the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> of 1413 required, and fighting soon broke out over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. In the Lutsk War of 1431 Švitrigaila allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> by the Treaty of Christmemel, but after Polish gains in Volhynia and the Knights' defeat at Dąbki he accepted a truce on 1 September 1431.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 31 August 1432 conspirators including Semen Olshanski, Petras Mangirdaitis and Jonas Goštautas attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a> at Ashmyany and installed Sigismund Kęstutaitis, the brother of Vytautas, who renewed the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> by the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> on 15 October 1432. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> split: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> backed Sigismund, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> stood by Švitrigaila. Sigismund won the Battle of Ašmena on 8 December 1432, and in May 1434 he granted Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> nobles a privilege guaranteeing their land rights.</p><p>To cut the Order's secret aid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> backed a Hussite invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1433, which ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and western Prussia; by the Truce of Łęczyca of 15 December 1433 the Knights promised to drop him, though the truce did not bind the Livonian Order. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> in May 1434 support resumed, and Švitrigaila's army under his nephew Sigismund Korybut met the Lithuanian and Polish army of Michael Žygimantaitis in September 1435. The battle, thought to have involved 30,000 men in all, ended in a heavy defeat for Švitrigaila and the Livonian Order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the Peace of Brześć Kujawski of 31 December 1435 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> agreed to support only grand dukes chosen jointly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and the Orders ceased to meddle in Polish and Lithuanian affairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a> offered on 4 September 1437 to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> for life under Poland, but at Sigismund's protest the Polish Senate refused, and in 1438 he withdrew to Moldavia. He returned in 1442 and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> until his death. The historians Jerzy Lukowski and Hubert Zawadzki date the end of the dispute to 1440, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> was accepted as ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithuanian Civil War (1432–1438)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
